{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Privacy notice — {{ product }}{% endblock %}
{% block description %}How {{ product }} processes personal data from public registers, and how to object.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<main class="legal">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {{ today }}. This notice covers people whose details appear in
      our lead data, and separately our own subscribers.</p>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>{{ legal.entity }} ({{ product }}) is the data controller.
      {%- if legal.address %} Registered address: {{ legal.address }}.{% endif %}
      You can reach us at <a href="mailto:{{ objection_email }}">{{ objection_email }}</a>.</p>

    <h2>If you are named in our data</h2>

    <h3>Where we got it</h3>
    <p>We did not get it from you. We take it from two public sources:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The Traffic Commissioners' <em>Applications &amp; Decisions</em> publications on GOV.UK,
        which are published as a matter of statute and licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0.</li>
      <li>Companies House, where a licence holder matches a registered company.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do not buy lists, and we do not collect anything about you from anywhere else.</p>

    <h3>What we hold</h3>
    <p>Only what those sources publish: your name; your role (director, partner, sole trader or
      transport manager); the operator's correspondence address and operating centres, which for a
      sole trader may be a home address; the licence number, type and authorised fleet; and the
      event that put you in the publication — an application, variation, decision or public inquiry.
      We hold no financial data, no contact details of yours beyond what the register publishes,
      and nothing that is not already public.</p>

    <h3>Why we process it, and on what basis</h3>
    <p>Our lawful basis is <strong>legitimate interests</strong> (UK GDPR Article 6(1)(f)). Our
      interest is running a business-to-business information service; our subscribers' interest is
      finding new commercial customers. We have assessed this against your interests and rights and
      concluded it is proportionate, because the data is already published by a public authority
      for the express purpose of making licensing decisions visible and open to objection, we
      publish only what that source publishes, and we act on any objection.</p>
    <p>Because we did not get the data from you, Article 14 applies: this notice is how we tell you
      we hold it. If you would like the full legitimate interests assessment, ask and we will send it.</p>

    <h3>Who sees it</h3>
    <p>Our subscribers — businesses that sell to hauliers — receive it as an email digest and a CSV.
      We tell them the data is public record, that they must comply with UK GDPR and PECR when
      contacting anyone in it, and we mark sole traders and partnerships so they are routed to
      phone or post rather than cold email. We do not sell your data on as a list and our terms
      forbid subscribers from redistributing it.</p>

    <h3>How long we keep it</h3>
    <p>We keep the source publications indefinitely, because they are the public record and we need
      them to show where any figure came from. Enrichment data drawn from Companies House is
      refreshed or removed on a twelve-month cycle. If you object, we keep the minimum needed to
      make sure you stay suppressed.</p>

    <h3>Your rights, and how to use them</h3>
    <p>You can ask us for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to correct it, ask us to delete
      it, ask us to restrict how we use it, and <strong>object to us processing it at all</strong>.
      Because we rely on legitimate interests, an objection is a strong right: tell us and we will
      stop, unless we can show compelling grounds that override yours.</p>
    <p>Email <a href="mailto:{{ objection_email }}">{{ objection_email }}</a>. We will act within
      30 days and add you to a suppression list so you do not reappear in a later digest. There is
      no charge and you do not have to give a reason.</p>
    <p>Note that we cannot remove you from the underlying GOV.UK publication — only the Traffic
      Commissioner can do that. We can only stop <em>our</em> processing.</p>
    <p>If we get it wrong, you can complain to the Information Commissioner's Office at
      <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk</a>.</p>

    <h2>If you are a subscriber</h2>
    <p>We hold your email address, company name, delivery preferences and subscription status. We
      process it to run your subscription — a contract basis — and we keep it while you subscribe
      and for six years afterwards for accounting. Payments are handled by Stripe; we never see
      your card details.</p>

    <h2>Where your data is processed</h2>
    <p>{{ legal.processors }}</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If we change how we process personal data we will update this notice and, where the change
      is significant, say so in the digest.</p>

    {%- if dry_run %}
    <hr>
    <p class="updated"><strong>Draft.</strong> This notice describes what the system actually does
      and is written to be honest rather than defensive, but it has not been reviewed by a
      solicitor. Have it checked before it goes live, and fill in the entity and processor details
      above.</p>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
</main>
{% endblock %}
